{0 Theming}

[silica-ui] (see {!page-ui}) ships a theme API in the module
[Silica_ui.Theme]. It uses only
the public API of Silica: a machine, two actions, a source and CSS. An app
could write it, but it does not have to.

{1:model Themes and tokens}

A token is a CSS value with a name, such as the page background or the corner
radius. A theme gives a value to each token and has a colour scheme ([Light] or
[Dark]), which sets the CSS [color-scheme] of form controls and scroll bars.

The tokens of [silica-ui] are the record [Silica_ui.Theme.tokens]. Because a
theme is a record value, a theme without a token does not compile. Four themes
come with the library: [light], [dark], [high-contrast] and [ocean]. A new theme
starts from one of them:

{@ocaml[
# open Silica_ui;;
# let paper =
    Theme.make ~name:"paper" ~label:"Paper" ~scheme:Light
      { (Theme.tokens Theme.light) with surface = "#fdfaf3"; raised = "#fffdf8" };;
val paper : Theme.t = <abstr>
# let set = Theme.set ~light:paper ~dark:Theme.dark [ paper; Theme.dark ];;
val set : Theme.set = <abstr>
]}

[Silica_ui.Theme.set] names the two themes that [System] uses: one for a
light system setting and one for a dark one. It checks the set and names the
problem:

{@ocaml[
# Theme.set ~light:Theme.dark ~dark:Theme.dark [ Theme.dark ];;
Exception:
Invalid_argument
 "Silica_ui.Theme: the light theme \"dark\" has a dark scheme".
# Theme.make ~name:"system" ~scheme:Light (Theme.tokens Theme.light);;
Exception: Invalid_argument "Silica_ui.Theme: bad theme name \"system\"".
]}

{1:css The CSS}

[Silica_ui.Theme.stylesheet] gives the CSS of a set. It has one block per
theme on the attribute [data-theme], one block for [System], and the Tailwind
map of the tokens:

{@ocaml[
# Theme.stylesheet set
  |> String.split_on_char '\n'
  |> List.filter (fun line -> String.ends_with ~suffix:"{" line);;
- : string list =
["@theme inline {"; "[data-theme=\"paper\"] {"; "[data-theme=\"dark\"] {";
 "[data-theme=\"system\"] {"; "@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {";
 "  [data-theme=\"system\"] {"]
]}

An app writes the stylesheet to [themes.css] with a small executable and a
dune rule, next to its Tailwind rule. Mini Linear has them in
[linear/client]:

From [linear/client/themes.ml]:
{@ocaml skip[
let () = print_string (Silica_ui.Theme.stylesheet Linear_theme.set)
]}

From [linear/client/dune]:
{@dune skip[
(executable
 (name themes)
 (modules themes)
 (libraries silica-ui silica_linear_app_native))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  themes.css
  (run ./themes.exe)))
]}

The Tailwind rule depends on [themes.css] and the Tailwind input imports it
(see {!page-recipes.tailwind}). A set that does not pass the checks of
[Silica_ui.Theme.set] stops the build.

The Tailwind names of the tokens are [ui-<token>] for colours ([bg-ui-surface],
[text-ui-muted], [border-ui-border], [bg-ui-accent], [text-ui-accent-text]),
[rounded-ui], [shadow-ui] and [font-ui]. The [spacing] token is the Tailwind
unit, so [p-4] is four times it. The map uses [@theme inline]: a utility reads
the CSS property ([var(--ui-surface)]) at the element itself, which a nested
theme needs. [silica-ui.css] gives each element with [data-theme] the surface
background, the text colour and the font.

Use tokens, not the Tailwind [dark:] variant: [dark:] follows only the system
setting and does not see the theme that the user chose.

{1:choose Choose, switch and keep}

The choice is [Silica_ui.Theme.choice]: [System], which follows the system
setting live, or [Fixed theme]. The theme component holds it. At the start and
at each change it sets [data-theme] on the [<html>] element with
{!Silica.Action.Set_document_attribute}. A change made in the page is also stored
in the cookie [silica-theme] for one year ({!Silica.Action.Set_cookie}).

Put one theme component in the page, here in the mini Linear header:

From [linear/app/shared/linear_app.mlx]:
{@ocaml skip[
      (Silica_ui.Theme.switcher
         (Silica_ui.Theme.props ~id:"theme" Linear_theme.set theme)
      )
]}

[Silica_ui.Theme.switcher] draws a [Silica_ui.Select] with [System] and each
theme. [Silica_ui.Theme.provider] has no view: the app draws its own switcher,
keeps the choice in its own state and gives it in the props. Both take
[?on_change], which hears the theme in use after each change, also when the
system setting changes under [System]. A component that needs the theme as a
value (a chart that draws with colours) gets it there. Other components need
nothing: the CSS changes.

{1:first-paint The first paint}

The server and the browser read the cookie before the first props, so both
start the theme component with the same choice. The server render writes the
[data-theme] of that choice on [<html>]:

From [linear/server/linear_server.ml]:
{@ocaml skip[
        Server.render_page_settled
          (Linear_app.make
             (Linear_theme.choice
                (Silica_server.cookie page.context.request
                   Silica_ui.Theme.cookie_name
                )
             )
          )
]}

From [linear/client/client_main.ml]:
{@ocaml skip[
  Silica_browser.mount_app ~root:"silica-root"
    (Linear_app.make
       (Linear_theme.choice (Silica_browser.cookie Silica_ui.Theme.cookie_name))
    )
]}

The server cannot know the system setting. For [System] the page has
[data-theme="system"], and the CSS gives the light theme there, and the dark
theme inside [@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)]. So the first frame has the
right colours with no script, and the page follows a change of the system
setting with no script too. The Playwright tests of the site read the
background of the first frame for both system settings, with and without
JavaScript.

A switch changes one attribute. The theme component handles one event, no
other component gets new props, and the browser changes the colours.

{1:scope A theme for a part of the page}

[Silica_ui.Theme.scope] puts a part of the view in another theme. Each page
of the component browser of the Silica site shows a story in each shipped
theme side by side:

{@ocaml[
# Silica.Render.to_string (Theme.scope Theme.ocean [ Silica.Node.text "Ocean" ]);;
- : string = "<div data-theme=\"ocean\">Ocean</div>"
]}

A popup in a scope (a dialog, popover, tooltip, menu, select, combobox or
command menu) uses the theme of the scope. Its portal paints it at the end of
the body, out of the scope element, so the browser renderer puts the
[data-theme] of the nearest scope on the portal container.

{1:tokens App tokens}

An app adds its own tokens with [~extra]. Write them as a record, so that a
theme without one does not compile, and turn the record into pairs in one
function. Mini Linear adds a warning colour:

From [linear/app/shared/linear_theme.ml]:
{@ocaml skip[
type warning = { warning : string; warning_surface : string }

let with_warning theme { warning; warning_surface } =
  Theme.make ~name:(Theme.name theme) ~label:(Theme.label theme)
    ~scheme:(Theme.scheme theme)
    ~extra:[ ("warning", warning); ("warning-surface", warning_surface) ]
    (Theme.tokens theme)
]}

The CSS property of an app token is [--<name>]. The Tailwind input maps it:

From [linear/client/input.css]:
{@css skip[
@theme inline {
  --color-warning: var(--warning);
  --color-warning-surface: var(--warning-surface);
}
]}

[Silica_ui.Theme.set] fails when a theme of the set does not have an app
token that another theme has.
